# Scoped to this subtree on purpose. The rest of Tools is Windows/.NET work
# where CRLF may be wanted; this directory is developed and run on macOS/Linux.

# Shell scripts must keep LF whatever the cloning machine's core.autocrlf says.
# With autocrlf=true the shebang becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and the script
# dies with "env: bash\r: No such file or directory".
* text=auto eol=lf

# These are the test corpus. Git must never rewrite a byte of them.
#
# Not a precaution -- verified: cloning with core.autocrlf=true corrupts seven
# of the PDFs (D01, D05, D07, D12, D13, D19, D21). They are reportlab output
# with uncompressed content streams, so they are mostly ASCII and git's
# text/binary heuristic classifies them as text, then rewrites LF to CRLF
# inside them. Tolerant readers repair the damage and render identically, which
# is precisely what makes it dangerous: the test set is silently altered and
# nothing complains. `text=auto` above does not save them; only this does.
*.pdf  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png  binary
*.tif  binary
*.tiff binary
